PUBLIC RESPONSE: {E}

The key issue with E under the NI 45-106 entity test (as of January 2021) is that the corporation’s most recent financial statements are not provided, so we cannot verify net assets of at least $5 M. Only the $5.5 M GIC is quantified; the investment properties that produced the $300 k liabilities have unknown carrying values and could easily push net assets below the threshold once fair-value or cost adjustments are applied. Without those statements the entity prong cannot be confirmed, and neither Alex nor Bob meets any individual or spousal test on the facts given.  

By contrast, A’s limited partnership satisfies the “all owners are accredited investors” route because the three limited partners each clear an individual threshold and the general-partner holding company received $6 M in cash from accredited individuals, placing its own net financial assets above $5 M. The other choices likewise meet clear individual-income, spousal-combined, or entity-net-asset tests on the explicit numbers supplied. E is therefore the only vehicle that cannot be classified as an accredited investor on the information given.}

